Model-based testing describes expected behavior as a small model. It generates action sequences and compares the real system with the model after each step.
It suits anything with history: sessions, retries, lifecycles.

[David Khourshid](/lore/david-khourshid) described the approach with state machines in [Model-Based Testing in React with State Machines](https://css-tricks.com/model-based-testing-in-react-with-state-machines/). XState builds it into `xstate/graph`: `createTestModel` wraps a machine, and `getShortestPaths` turns it into test paths. "Path generation enables model-based testing - generating test cases directly from your state machine." [Graph & Paths](https://stately.ai/docs/graph)

Effect 4's `Arbitrary.array` generates command sequences. Its documentation uses a state machine as the example, and failed sequences shrink by removing irrelevant commands. [Arbitrary.ts](https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect/blob/67ba4e46a11ccda0b6761578bfd22c04ae00167d/packages/effect/src/Arbitrary.ts)

In rat-stack, [the visitor identity model](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/main/packages/events/test/visitor.model.test.ts) generates visits from three devices.
Visits may keep or drop the cookie. Visits also cross day boundaries.

A persistent id survives every visit that keeps its cookie. A daily id stays stable within a day and sets no cookie.
It never repeats on a later day. Making the daily hash ignore the day broke the model at once.

Machines in this repo are [lifecycles](/lore/lifecycles-are-machines), so each one is a ready-made test model. See [property-based testing](/lore/property-based-testing) for single-input rules.

See also: [Tests through supplied services](/lore/tests-that-earn-their-place).
